   "tutor": "Here is the rule: after he, she or it, the verb takes -s, -es (watches) or -ies (cries).\nRight: \"The robot washes the floor.\" Wrong: \"The robot wash the floor.\" The subject is it, so the plain verb without an ending is not correct.\nLet me think aloud with one example: \"The robot fixes cars.\" Step 1: the robot is it. Step 2: fix ends in x. Step 3: verbs ending in x take -es.\nNow your turn. Check the subject and the verb's last letter, then write the verb:\nWrite the correct form: My sister ___ (play) tennis on Sundays. (answer with only the verb)",
